e('The program never notices.','On successful resolution the instruction resumes; the program can still observe latency and fault counters, and some faults fail.','Faultnotinvisibleguarantee.')
e('A segmentation fault means the address fails the VMA check. Every other fault is the kernel doing its job lazily.','Missing or incompatible VMAs are common SIGSEGV causes, but the VMA check is not the whole story. File faults can deliver SIGBUS; allocation can fail; architecture-specific protection and special mappings have additional rules.','Falseallotherfaultlazy distinction.')
e('- A **minor fault** is resolved without I/O: zero a fresh frame, or map a page that is already in RAM (in the page cache, or shared with another process). Cost: roughly a microsecond or less.','- A **minor fault** is accounted as resolved without the page-in I/O required by a major fault, for example demand-zero allocation or mapping resident backing. Its handler may still incur allocation, reclaim or contention work.','Nofixedlatency/ornoanyI/O.',gr)
e('- A **major fault** must read from storage: a file page not yet cached, or a page that was swapped out. Cost: tens to hundreds of microseconds on NVMe, around 10 ms on a spinning disk.','- A **major fault** is accounted as requiring page-in I/O, for example missing file or swap backing. Latency depends on caching, device, queueing and kernel behavior.','No unsourceddevicetiming.',gr)
e('That is a gap of four to five orders of magnitude, so it matters which kind you are taking.','Fault type is useful evidence, but it does not alone determine duration.\n\n> [!note] Content gap\n> Fixed minor/major fault latencies are omitted because no reproducible benchmark configuration is provided. Times in the arithmetic exercise below are hypothetical inputs.','Originalrangecontradictsclaimedratio.',gr)
e('When a program calls `malloc` for a big block, glibc calls `mmap`, and the kernel only creates a VMA. No frames are allocated and no PTEs are filled. The first touch of each page takes a minor fault that allocates and zeroes one 4 KiB frame.','A large glibc allocation may use anonymous mmap, depending on allocator state and tunables. An ordinary new anonymous mapping without prefaulting can initially reserve a virtual region with backing populated lazily. Under a base-page model, first writes allocate zero-filled backing; page size, THP and prior population affect the fault count.','mallocnotalwaysnewmmap/4KiB.',mm)
e('#include <sys/resource.h>','#include <sys/resource.h>\n#include <unistd.h>','Getruntimepagesize.')
e('    long before = minflt();\n    for (size_t i = 0; i < len; i += 4096)\n        p[i] = 1;','    if (p == MAP_FAILED) {\n        perror("mmap");\n        return 1;\n    }\n    long step = sysconf(_SC_PAGESIZE);\n    if (step <= 0) return 1;\n    long before = minflt();\n    for (size_t i = 0; i < len; i += step)\n        ((volatile char *)p)[i] = 1;','Checkmapping/pagesize andensurestoresobservable.')
e('           minflt() - before);\n}','           minflt() - before);\n    munmap(p, len);\n}','Releaseexamplemapping.')
e('A **read** of an untouched anonymous page maps the kernel\'s single shared **zero page**, read-only. Only a later write allocates a real frame. Reading a huge calloc\'d array costs almost no RAM.','On common Linux configurations, reads of untouched anonymous mappings can use read-only shared zero backing. Architecture, THP and mapping flags affect this; page tables and metadata still cost memory, and calloc may have allocator-specific behavior.','No universalzeropage/zeroRAM.',th)
e('`MAP_POPULATE` (or `mlock`) faults everything in up front, trading a slow start for no faults later. Latency-sensitive systems do this.','`MAP_POPULATE` requests prefaulting but does not guarantee full population or future residency. Successful ordinary `mlock` keeps the range resident, whereas `mlock2(MLOCK_ONFAULT)` is lazy. Neither removes every later COW/protection fault, especially across fork.','Prefaultnotallfaultsgone.',ml+mm)
e('1. Copies the parent\'s page tables (not the pages).\n2. Marks every private writable page **read-only in both** parent and child, and bumps each page\'s reference count.\n3. Returns. Both processes now share every frame.','1. Creates the child\'s memory context and mappings, respecting exceptions such as DONTFORK/WIPEONFORK.\n2. Shares eligible private backing under COW and write-protects the required mappings; some page-table work can be deferred.\n3. Leaves shared mappings shared. Certain pinned/private pages may need special copying rather than ordinary COW.','NotallVMAs/everypage/everyPTEcopied.',fk+ma)
e('Memory grows only by the pages the parent modifies during the save. A write-heavy workload can nearly double RSS,','COW can add backing for modified pages, alongside child buffers, new parent allocations and page tables. A write-heavy workload can substantially increase total physical memory use,','RSSeachprocesscounts sharedpages;notonlymodifiedgrowth.')
e('Each later write fault costs a copy. `posix_spawn` or `vfork` avoid both when the child will just `exec`.','Later writes may copy backing or reuse it when exclusive. Suitable posix_spawn implementations can avoid copying the parent\'s address space for exec; vfork has strict child-side restrictions and is not a drop-in fork replacement.','Alreadyexclusivecopyexception/spawnimplementation.',fk)
e('Nothing is read at `mmap` time; each page loads on its first fault.','For ordinary mappings without prefaulting, population is lazy; MAP_POPULATE, existing page-cache state and other flags can change when work occurs.','Notallmmaplazy.',mm)
e('If another process already read the file, the fault is minor; if not, it is major.','Resident, up-to-date cached backing can avoid page-in I/O. Readahead, eviction and fault-around mean neither process history nor one page implies a fixed count/type of faults.','Readhistorynotproofresident.',mm+gr)
e('- **Durability needs `msync`.** Writes to a shared mapping reach disk when the kernel gets round to it. Call `msync(MS_SYNC)` (or `fsync`) for a guarantee.','- **Durability needs explicit synchronization.** Check successful msync(MS_SYNC) or the applicable fsync protocol for file data; file creation/rename may also need directory synchronization. Hardware and filesystem guarantees still apply.','Notunqualifieddurabilityguarantee.',mm)
e('Anonymous memory (heap, stack) has no file behind it, so without swap the kernel cannot evict it at all.','Preserving live anonymous data during reclaim normally requires swap backing. This is different from freeing or explicitly discarding unneeded anonymous pages, which can happen without swap.','MADVFREE/DONTNEEDunmapexceptions.',ma)
e('A later access faults, and the kernel reads the slot back: a major fault.','A later access resolves the swap entry. It can use resident swap-cache or compressed backing; a storage read is not inevitable.','Swapcache/zswapnotalwaysdisk.',zs)
e('Android, ChromeOS and Fedora use it by default.','Several operating-system distributions use it; exact defaults depend on version and configuration. Optional zram writeback can use a backing device.','Defaultsunstable;zramdiskoption.',zr)
e('Compression costs microseconds of CPU rather than milliseconds of I/O.','Compression trades CPU and metadata for saved backing capacity; cost and compression ratio depend on data and configuration.','Unsupportedtiming.',zr+zs)
e('- **Stack growth**: the stack VMA grows downward when a fault lands just below it.','- **Stack growth**: eligible grow-down stack mappings may expand within limits; an arbitrary below-stack fault does not guarantee expansion.','Needeligibility/limit.')
e('- Linux checks the address against the VMAs: outside any VMA, or against its permissions, is SIGSEGV; anything else is lazy work.','- Linux consults VMAs and backing state. Invalid accesses often deliver SIGSEGV; file failures may deliver SIGBUS, and other resource/protection failures are possible.','Summarysamecorrection.')
e('- Minor faults need no I/O (around a microsecond); major faults read storage (tens of microseconds to milliseconds). A tiny major-fault rate can dominate EAT.','- Minor/major counters distinguish page-in work, not fixed latency. An expensive fault can dominate a hypothetical average even at a low rate.','Drop unsupportedbenchmark.',gr)
e('- Anonymous pages can only be evicted to swap; the swap location is stored in the not-present PTE.','- Preserving anonymous contents during reclaim normally needs swap backing; discard/free are different. Swap entries identify backing that may be cached or compressed.','Summaryconsistent.',ma+zs)
f(1,'prompt','On x86-64 Linux, an actual user-mode load instruction accesses virtual address 0x10, which has no mapping or VMA. What normally happens?','AvoidISO Cundefinedbehaviorpredictguarantee.')
