f(2,'prompt','A scanner maps and reads an entire ordinary 2 GiB file. On a second fresh mapping, all its pages remain resident and up to date in the page cache, with no concurrent eviction or modification. How do faults that populate the second mapping normally differ from cold-storage faults?','grep usesreadnotnecessarilymmap;originalanswerunsupported.')
f(2,'workedExample','A cold file mapping can require storage I/O to obtain its backing. Under the second-run assumptions, the file data is already resident, so faults that populate the new page tables can be minor. Readahead/fault-around can map multiple pages per fault. Ordinary read-based tools such as grep need not generate file-data page faults at all; their storage reads and anonymous buffer faults must be distinguished.','CriticalreadIOvs mappedfaultdistinction.',mm+gr)
